# Phones and installed web apps

Applies to any interface that can be used on a phone, and always to one that ships a web app
manifest, because that app can be installed to the home screen. The design workflow decides a
screen; this rule keeps its edges usable on the device. It is the build pattern and the checks for
the Mock2 constitution's design principle §13 (designed for the device and the mode it is used in),
never a gate (Rule 0). An app with no bottom-anchored control needs no script; a desktop-only app
needs none of this.

Why it exists (Nodus R-041, 2026-09-30–2026-10-01): an installed Android app with
three-button navigation clipped its bottom controls while the browser tab was correct. The first
safe-area fix passed emulation and an initial device check, then failed on a later launch. A
blanket fallback subsequently left excess space on launches where the browser already kept the
page clear. On reload, a CSS viewport-height shell could also outgrow the visible area. Nodus W33
now measures the visible bottom, recalculates it on reload and resume with bounded settling
retries, and subtracts space already excluded from the remaining inset. The owner reported success
on 2026-10-01. That report does not establish a repeated cold-start/reload/resume matrix, other
devices, or the exact browser mechanism; record those checks separately.

## What changes when the app is installed

In a browser tab the browser's own bars usually keep the page clear of the system bars. Installed
(`display: standalone` or `fullscreen`), Android Chrome can draw the app edge-to-edge, and on iOS
with `viewport-fit=cover` the page runs under the home indicator. The viewport, and with it
`100dvh`, `100vh`, `inset: 0` and `position: fixed; bottom: 0`, then extends behind the system
navigation, where a tap goes to the system and not to the app. With `viewport-fit=cover`, iOS
Safari and recent Chrome on Android can also draw a tab under the system bars as their own bars
collapse, and they report the inset when they do. A layout that is right in the tab is not verified
for the installed app until the installed view has been checked.

The browser is meant to report the covered strip as `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`. Some Android
devices draw the installed app behind their navigation buttons and report 0, or less than the bar.
Build for both.

## Build

1. **Viewport.** `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">`,
   added together with the inset padding below, never alone. Without `viewport-fit=cover`, iOS
   reports every safe-area inset as 0. With it and no padding, content runs under the notch in
   landscape and under the system bars, which is worse than before.
2. **One inset variable**, defined once, used by everything anchored to an edge:

   ```css
   :root {
     --app-height: 100vh;
     --reported-inset: max(env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px), env(safe-area-max-inset-bottom, 0px));
     --viewport-bottom-gap: 0px;
     --bottom-inset: max(0px, calc(max(var(--reported-inset), var(--nav-fallback, 0px))
                                      - var(--viewport-bottom-gap)));
   }
   @supports (height: 100dvh) { :root { --app-height: 100dvh; } }
   .app-shell, .full-screen-sheet {
     position: relative; height: var(--app-height); box-sizing: border-box;
   }
   .bottom-bar { padding-bottom: calc(10px + var(--bottom-inset)); }
   .page { padding-left: max(16px, env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px));
           padding-right: max(16px, env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px)); }
   .fab { position: absolute; bottom: calc(16px + var(--bottom-inset)); }
   ```

   - Give every `env()` a fallback (`, 0px`). A name the browser does not know, with no fallback,
     makes the whole declaration invalid at computed-value time: the property drops to its initial
     value (0 for padding) and takes the `max()` minimum with it. `safe-area-max-inset-bottom` is
     new (Chrome 135), so an older or non-Chromium browser is exactly that case.
   - `safe-area-max-inset-bottom` is the largest value the inset can take while the browser's bars
     show and hide; the larger of the two keeps the padding steady.
   - The sample shells start at y = 0 and contain their bottom controls. Keep padding inside the
     measured height with border-box sizing; the floating example is absolute within that shell,
     rather than fixed to an independently taller viewport.
   - Subtract excluded bottom space only when all consumers use the measured shell described below.
     The gap is an observation about that shell, not a general claim that the system bar was measured.
   - A bar pads, a floating element moves. A bottom bar, sheet or footer takes the inset into its
     padding, so its background still reaches the screen edge and its controls sit above the system
     bar. A floating button or toast is offset instead (`bottom: calc(16px + var(--bottom-inset))`).
3. **Everything anchored to the bottom uses it:** fixed or sticky bottom bars and toolbars, bottom
   sheets and full-screen dialogs on phones, sticky action footers, bottom toasts, floating buttons,
   and the last row of a `100dvh` scroll area. The sides take `safe-area-inset-left` / `-right` (in
   landscape the notch, and three-button navigation, sit at a side), and the top takes
   `safe-area-inset-top` under `display: fullscreen` or a translucent status bar. The defect behind
   this rule was the bottom edge; verify the others where the app uses them.
4. **Android that reports too little.** A fixed navigation fallback is a project decision,
   not a universal measurement of Android's navigation bar. The reference below keeps a raw 48 px
   fallback in installed Android portrait mode; the shared CSS takes the larger of that and the
   reported inset, then subtracts only the bottom gap already excluded from the measured shell.
   A 48 px gap and a 48 px fallback therefore leave no *additional* inset. Do not add reported,
   fallback and excluded space together. Keep the bar's own design padding additive.

   If the window already stops above the system bar but there is no visual-viewport gap to measure,
   this conservative fallback may still leave excess space. Record its conditions, value, price and
   revisit trigger in `state/decisions.md`. Change it from device evidence, with pure-function
   tests, rather than silently relaxing a condition after one good launch. Nodus's separate
   screen-minus-window threshold (60 px, DEC-041) is project evidence, not a portable safe-area API.
   Neither 48 px nor that threshold is a general design principle. Landscape, gesture navigation,
   tablets and foldables need their own evidence. CPR current §O.3 governs the decision's status.

5. **A viewport-sized shell owns a fresh height.** Start with `100vh` and `100dvh` as above.
   Where device evidence shows a mismatch, measure the current window and visual viewport rather
   than caching launch dimensions or trusting a CSS unit indefinitely. For a shell whose top is
   anchored at layout-viewport y = 0, use
   `min(innerHeight, visualViewport.height + visualViewport.offsetTop)` at scale 1; its excluded
   bottom gap is `innerHeight - measuredHeight`. Validate finite, positive dimensions; ignore
   pinch zoom for layout sizing; use `innerHeight` when VisualViewport is missing or invalid;
   retain the last valid height during a zero-height transient. A translated or scrolled shell
   must account for its own origin; do not copy this root-shell formula into it unchanged.

   Apply that height to the shell and any viewport-sized sheet sharing the inset variable.
   Every bottom surface using the net inset must be anchored to this same measured bottom;
   subtracting a gap while leaving a footer attached to a taller viewport can clip it again.
   A normally scrolling page does not need a fixed shell just to adopt this rule. Decide explicitly
   whether the keyboard should shrink the shell or leave a scrollable form, and verify that policy.

6. **Reload and resume are lifecycle events.** Install the watcher once in the browser after the
   document exists, outside component renders; call its cleanup when that owner is removed.
   Recalculate immediately on startup, `load`, `pageshow` (including a back/forward-cache restore),
   visible resume, orientation, fullscreen and display-mode changes. Also respond to window resize
   and VisualViewport resize/scroll. At each lifecycle event, replace pending retries, update now,
   on the next animation frame, and at a bounded set of later times: browser bars can settle without
   a resize event. The 250/1000/3000 ms below are the Nodus settling schedule, not guaranteed browser
   timings; tune from evidence. Do not poll forever or leave old listeners/timers after cleanup.

   ```ts
   export const NAV_BAR_PX = 48;
   export function navFallback(o: {
     standalone: boolean; android: boolean; portrait: boolean;
   }): number {
     return o.standalone && o.android && o.portrait ? NAV_BAR_PX : 0;
   }
   export function readViewport(win: Window): { height: number; bottomGap: number } {
     const windowHeight = win.innerHeight;
     if (!Number.isFinite(windowHeight) || windowHeight <= 0) return { height: 0, bottomGap: 0 };
     const vv = win.visualViewport;
     const bottom = vv && vv.scale === 1 && Number.isFinite(vv.height) && vv.height > 0
       && Number.isFinite(vv.offsetTop) && vv.offsetTop >= 0
       ? vv.height + vv.offsetTop : windowHeight;
     const height = Math.min(windowHeight, bottom);
     return { height, bottomGap: windowHeight - height };
   }
   export function watchViewport(win: Window = window): () => void {
     const doc = win.document;
     const modes = ['(display-mode: standalone)', '(display-mode: fullscreen)']
       .map(q => win.matchMedia(q));
     const update = () => {
       const viewport = readViewport(win);
       if (viewport.height <= 0) return;
       const standalone = modes.some(m => m.matches)
         || (win.navigator as Navigator & { standalone?: boolean }).standalone === true;
       const fallback = navFallback({
         standalone, android: /Android/.test(win.navigator.userAgent),
         portrait: win.innerHeight > win.innerWidth,
       });
       const style = doc.documentElement.style;
       style.setProperty('--app-height', viewport.height + 'px');
       style.setProperty('--viewport-bottom-gap', viewport.bottomGap + 'px');
       style.setProperty('--nav-fallback', fallback + 'px');
     };
     let frame: number | null = null;
     let timers: number[] = [];
     const cancel = () => {
       if (frame !== null) { win.cancelAnimationFrame(frame); frame = null; }
       timers.forEach(timer => win.clearTimeout(timer));
       timers = [];
     };
     const settle = () => {
       cancel();
       update();
       frame = win.requestAnimationFrame(() => { frame = null; update(); });
       timers = [250, 1000, 3000].map(ms => win.setTimeout(update, ms));
     };
     const visible = () => { if (doc.visibilityState === 'visible') settle(); };
     const lifecycle = ['load', 'pageshow', 'orientationchange'] as const;
     win.addEventListener('resize', update);
     win.visualViewport?.addEventListener('resize', update);
     win.visualViewport?.addEventListener('scroll', update);
     lifecycle.forEach(event => win.addEventListener(event, settle));
     doc.addEventListener('visibilitychange', visible);
     doc.addEventListener('fullscreenchange', settle);
     modes.forEach(mode => mode.addEventListener('change', settle));
     settle();
     return () => {
       cancel();
       win.removeEventListener('resize', update);
       win.visualViewport?.removeEventListener('resize', update);
       win.visualViewport?.removeEventListener('scroll', update);
       lifecycle.forEach(event => win.removeEventListener(event, settle));
       doc.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', visible);
       doc.removeEventListener('fullscreenchange', settle);
       modes.forEach(mode => mode.removeEventListener('change', settle));
     };
   }
   ```

## Verify

The phone project of the browser harness renders the installed view as well as the tab. With
Playwright on Chromium:

```ts
const context = await browser.newContext({
  viewport: { width: 384, height: 820 }, isMobile: true, hasTouch: true,
  userAgent: 'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 15; SM-S928B) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/141.0.0.0 Mobile Safari/537.36',
});
await context.addInitScript(({ standalone, outside }) => {
  const real = window.matchMedia.bind(window);
  window.matchMedia = (q: string) => {
    const m = real(q);
    if (q.replace(/\s/g, '') === '(display-mode:standalone)') {
      Object.defineProperty(m, 'matches', { get: () => standalone });
    }
    return m;
  };
  // How much of the screen the page does not cover: 37 is the status bar alone.
  Object.defineProperty(screen, 'height', { get: () => window.innerHeight + outside });
}, { standalone: true, outside: 37 });
const page = await context.newPage();
// The inset the browser reports; leave it out for the Android case that reports 0.
await (await context.newCDPSession(page)).send('Emulation.setSafeAreaInsetsOverride',
  { insets: { bottom: 34, bottomMax: 34 } });
```

- Cases: browser tab and installed Android/iOS; zero, small, large and max-only reported
  inset; VisualViewport equal to, shorter than or taller than the window; missing/invalid viewport;
  a nonzero offset; zero-height transients; pinch zoom. With a raw 48 px fallback and reported 16 px,
  the unexcluded inset is 48, never 64. With a measured bottom gap of 48 it is 0, leaving only the
  bar's own padding; with a gap of 28 it is 20. A larger reported inset still wins before the
  subtraction. Where a fallback deliberately leaves space, check its recorded price too.
- Lifecycle cases: finish the initial settling window, then dispatch `pageshow` or a visible
  `visibilitychange`; change the available height later without dispatching resize, and assert that
  the renewed retries update the shell. Cover load, orientation, fullscreen/display-mode change,
  window and visual resize/scroll, repeated lifecycle events replacing rather than stacking timers,
  hidden visibility not restarting them, and cleanup removing timers/listeners.
- Assert the shell's measured bottom and control clearance against the visible bottom, as well as
  the window bottom, rather than checking padding alone. For each opened bottom bar, sheet, footer,
  toast or floating control, compare its rectangle with the appropriate safe edge. Check upper
  bounds too: the already-excluded-gap case must not reserve the same inset a second time. Keep the
  bar's design padding in the expectation. A surface the journey does not open is not behaviorally
  verified merely because its computed padding looks right.
- The stub reaches script only: CSS `@media (display-mode: standalone)` rules still match as
  `browser`, and `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` does not emulate `display-mode` (Chromium 141).
  Record display-mode CSS as not verified.
- `Emulation.setSafeAreaInsetsOverride` is a Chromium DevTools command (it worked on Chromium 141);
  WebKit and Firefox projects cover the tab only. Emulation is a substitute measurement: record it
  as one, attach a screenshot of the emulated installed view to the change record, and record the
  check on a real device as not verified until someone has done it (CPR current §O.2, §M.3). The
  device check includes a cold start: close the app fully (swipe it away from recent apps), open it
  again and look again, more than once. Also reload in each mode, background and resume the app,
  rotate it, and test short viewports and the keyboard policy. Re-check after the bars have settled,
  recording browser/OS version and navigation mode where known. The first fix passed its initial
  look and failed on a later launch; the W33 owner's success report is not this whole matrix.
- Fail first against the build from before the fix, and make sure that build really is the old
  code. Delete the build output, and check that the before build exits 0 and emits a different
  bundle hash: a failed build step can leave the new bundle in place, and the browser test then
  passes against it, a false "before". In Nodus a new unit test importing the new function broke
  the old build that way.
